The correct patterns, verified:

```js
// POST with JSON — the three musts
const res = await fetch('/api/users', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },  // must set explicitly
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Al' }),                // must stringify
});

// THE trap: 404/500 do NOT throw
const res = await fetch('/api/missing');
res.ok;        // false (status 404)
res.status;    // 404
// fetch did NOT reject — you must check:
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);

// Cancellation — AbortController
const ctrl = new AbortController();
const req = fetch('/api/slow', { signal: ctrl.signal });
ctrl.abort();  // req rejects with AbortError ✓ (verified)
// the production pattern: abort on unmount / new search / timeout

// Response body — a one-shot stream
const data = await res.json();   // consumes the stream
// await res.json() again → TypeError: body already read
```

The three-layer error model (the interview structure):
1. Network/refused/CORS/aborted → fetch itself rejects → .catch
2. HTTP error status (404, 500) → fetch RESOLVES → check res.ok / res.status
3. Body parse issues (invalid JSON) → res.json() rejects

Production wrapper (what a complete answer looks like):
```js
async function api(path, opts) {
  const res = await fetch(path, opts);
  if (!res.ok) {
    const err = new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    err.status = res.status;
    try { err.body = await res.json(); } catch {}
    throw err;
  }
  return res.json();
}
```

When to reach for a library: React Query/SWR add caching, dedupe, retry, background refresh — the things fetch leaves you to build.
